(New). Definition of terms
Terms used in this Law shall have the following meanings:












1. Good health refers to a state of complete physical, mental, intellectual and social
well-being; and not merely the absence of disease or disability;
2. Hazard risk to health refers to an act or lifestyle or environment without proper
hygiene that may cause sickness, diseases or disability;
3. Nutrition refers to an enchained process stating from production, distribution,
cooking until consumption of beneficial and safe food for physical and
intellectual growth and development;
4. Nuisance refers to an act which caused harmful effects to healthy, physical, and
mental of human beings and society, such as: vibration, light, color, odor, smoke,
dust of over standards;
5. Health care facility refer to hospitals of all levels, dispensaries, clinics and
pharmacies;
6. Youth refers to person whose age is from eighteen to twenty four years;
7. Reproductive age refers to person whose age is from fifteen to forty nine years;
8. Elderly person refers to person who is aging from and over sixty years;
9. Formol refers to chemical solution injected into a corpse to prevent its perishment
and bad odor;
10. Building facility refers to residential houses together with their surrounded
facilities, including boats or rafts permanently using for familial residences,
vehicles with bedrooms and toilets, offices, guesthouses, hotels and others;
11. Pollution refers to state of environment with material, biological and chemical
alteration due to the contamination of substances or various elements in water,
soil, air over the determined environmental quality standards;
12. Detention places refer to detention room of a police station, prisons, detention
camps and re-education centers.

(Amended). Elements of Hygiene
Hygiene is comprised of:
− Hygiene of persons, families or communities;
− Hygiene of drinking and domestic water, food and consumption goods;
− Hygiene of buildings, hotels, guesthouses and restaurants;
− Hygiene of schools and other educational institutions;
− Hygiene of health care facilities;












− Hygiene of labor and occupation, production, construction and
maintenance sites and industries;
− Hygiene of public places, markets, funerals;
− Environmental health;
− Hygiene of detention places.

(Amended). Elements of Health Promotion
Health promotion includes:
− Health education;
− Mother and child health promotion;
− Youth and reproductive health promotion;
− Health promotion for elderly, handicapped and disabled persons;
− Nutrition;
− Sports, gymnastics and arts;
− Arrangement of public parks and green spaces, rest and touristic areas.

(New). Prohibitions for Individuals, Legal Entities or Organizations
Individuals, legal entities or organizations are prohibited to act as follows:
1. Create obstruction, do not cooperate with health officers or staffs whilst
they perform their duties;
2. Use of violence, threat, obstruct the duty performance of health officers or
staffs;
3. Give the bribes to health officers or staffs in relation with the area of
health activities;
4. Throw waste, rubbish, animal corpse, dirt or chemical substance in  or
release waste water from manufactory or from other production facilities
into water sources or other places without treatment process;
5. Do not allow an infected person to work on food processing and services
in order to avoid the disease spread;
6. Consume food which will be hazardous to health, such as: eating meat of
infected animals, perishable food, food with toxic chemical substance and
others;
7. Keep corpses without formol injection or corpses of persons infected
communicable diseases; incinerate or bury the corpses nearby the
communities or water sources;
8. Grow and leave animals without attendance in public places;












9. Violate marketing rules on products which are substituted mother
breastfeeding;
10. Have other behaviors contradicted with the laws and regulations on
hygiene, disease prevention and health promotion.

Section 7
Management and Inspection

(New). Rights and Duties of Ministry of Health
To manage and inspect the activities on hygiene, disease prevention and health
promotion, the Ministry of Health has the following rights and duties:
1. Develop the policies, strategic plans, laws and regulations on the management
and inspection of hygiene, disease prevention and health promotion for the
submission to the Government for consideration;
2. Translate the above mentioned policies and strategic plans into its own
detailed plans, programs and projects, and provide direction of their
implementation;
3. Advertise, disseminate and give education the laws and regulations on
hygiene, disease prevention and health promotion;
4. Suspend or cancel the decisions, orders, instructions and notices of  the
management and inspection organizations at lower levels under its own
responsibility on hygiene, disease prevention and health promotion which are
contradicted with the laws and regulations;
5. Determine the standard of health village model;
6. Consider and handle the propositions made by individuals, legal entities or
organizations relating to the issues on hygiene, disease prevention and health
promotion;












7. Coordinate with all parties and sectors concerned in management and
inspection of food hygiene, and drinking  and domestic water quality for the
effective implementation;
8. Build, improve and train the health officers on hygiene, disease prevention
and health promotion;
9.  Cooperate with foreign countries and international organizations to facilitate
the management and inspection of hygiene, disease prevention and health
promotion;
10. Regularly summarize and report the results of the implementation of activities
on hygiene, disease prevention and health promotion for the submission to the
Government;
11. Exercise other rights and perform other duties as provided for in the laws and
regulations.
